Type "bt" to get an explanation of how the code ended up there (a
standard step in debugging, although I can probably guess). Also (if
the debugging symbols are in place, but you seem to be running the
original NetHack rather than your debug-compiled one) you can use "list"
in order to see the exact context in the code where the code went wrong.
Look for values that are unexpectedly zero.

As for rescuing the game, the main thing I can think of right now would
be rerolling the level. This requires a patch to the game in order to
remove trickeries (like, say, AceHack has); then you could modify the
game loading code to not load Water. It'd be a mess, but not 100%
impossible to pull off.

(Incidentally, Water is the reason that multiplayer AceHack games are
singleplayer through the Planes; the bubble movement code is really
complex and I didn't manage to synchronize it between players.)

